(>> 1.1 Fundamental Circuit Quantities and Basic Laws) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Direct Current \(DC\) circuits form the foundational basis for power supplies,) Tj
T*
(instrumentation, and bioelectrical measurement interfaces.) Tj
T*
(* Circuit Definitions: Node \(junction where 2 or more elements connect\), Essential) Tj
T*
(Node \(junction of 3 or more elements\), Branch \(path connecting two nodes containing) Tj
T*
(an element\), Mesh \(a loop containing no other loops inside it\).) Tj
T*
(* Ohm's Law: Current I through a linear conductor between two points is directly) Tj
T*
(proportional to voltage V: V = I * R, where R is resistance in Ohms \(Omega\).) Tj
T*
(Resistivity relation: R = rho * L / A.) Tj
T*
(* Kirchhoff's Current Law \(KCL\): The algebraic sum of currents entering any) Tj
T*
(electrical node is identically zero: sum I_entering = sum I_leaving. Grounded in the) Tj
T*
(Law of Conservation of Charge.) Tj
T*
(* Kirchhoff's Voltage Law \(KVL\): The algebraic sum of all potential differences) Tj
T*
(around any closed circuit loop is identically zero: sum V_drops = sum V_rises.) Tj
T*
(Grounded in the Law of Conservation of Energy.) Tj
T*
(* Mesh \(Loop\) Analysis: Based on KVL. Formulates a system of linear equations) Tj
T*
([R_mesh] * [I_mesh] = [V_mesh] where R_ii is the self-resistance of mesh i, and R_ij) Tj
T*
(is the mutual resistance shared between mesh i and mesh j.) Tj
T*
(* Nodal Analysis: Based on KCL. Formulates [G_node] * [V_node] = [I_node] where) Tj
T*
(diagonal elements are the sum of conductances attached to node i, and off-diagonal) Tj
T*
(elements are negative mutual conductances.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 1.2 Network Theorems for Linear Bilateral Circuits) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Superposition Theorem: In any linear network containing multiple independent) Tj
T*
(sources, the voltage across \(or current through\) any element is the algebraic sum of) Tj
T*
(the voltages \(or currents\) caused by each independent source acting alone, with all) Tj
T*
(other independent sources deactivated \(voltage sources replaced by short circuits,) Tj
T*
(current sources replaced by open circuits\). Dependent sources remain active.) Tj
T*
(* Thevenin's Theorem: Any linear, two-terminal bilateral network consisting of) Tj
T*
(sources and resistances can be completely replaced by an equivalent circuit) Tj
T*
(containing a single ideal voltage source \(V_th\) in series with an equivalent) Tj
T*
(resistance \(R_th\). V_th is the open-circuit voltage across terminals \(V_oc\); R_th is) Tj
T*
(the equivalent resistance seen looking into the terminals with all independent) Tj
T*
(sources deactivated.) Tj
T*
(* Norton's Theorem: Dual of Thevenin's theorem. Any two-terminal linear network can) Tj
T*
(be replaced by a single ideal current source \(I_N\) in parallel with an equivalent) Tj
T*
(resistance \(R_N\). I_N is the short-circuit current across terminals \(I_sc\); R_N =) Tj
T*
(R_th. Source transformation relation: V_th = I_N * R_th.) Tj
T*
(* Maximum Power Transfer Theorem: A linear DC network delivers maximum electrical) Tj
T*
