T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: In a two-mesh circuit, Mesh 1 has a 20V battery in series with a 4 Omega) Tj
T*
(resistor, connected to a common branch containing a 6 Omega resistor. Mesh 2 connects) Tj
T*
(the 6 Omega common resistor to an 8 Omega resistor and a 12V battery. Determine the loop) Tj
T*
(currents I1, I2 and the current through the 6 Omega shared branch.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Set up KVL mesh equations:) Tj
T*
(Mesh 1 \(clockwise current I1\):) Tj
T*
(20 - 4*I1 - 6*\(I1 - I2\) = 0 => 10*I1 - 6*I2 = 20.) Tj
T*
(Mesh 2 \(clockwise current I2\):) Tj
T*
(- 6*\(I2 - I1\) - 8*I2 - 12 = 0 => - 6*I1 + 14*I2 = -12.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Solve the system using Cramer's rule:) Tj
T*
(Delta = det\([ 10 -6 ; -6 14 ]\) = \(10\)\(14\) - \(-6\)\(-6\) = 140 - 36 = 104.) Tj
T*
(Delta_1 = det\([ 20 -6 ; -12 14 ]\) = \(20\)\(14\) - \(-12\)\(-6\) = 280 - 72 = 208.) Tj
T*
(Delta_2 = det\([ 10 20 ; -6 -12 ]\) = \(10\)\(-12\) - \(-6\)\(20\) = -120 + 120 = 0.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Compute currents:) Tj
T*
(I1 = Delta_1 / Delta = 208 / 104 = 2.0 Amperes.) Tj
T*
(I2 = Delta_2 / Delta = 0 / 104 = 0.0 Amperes.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 4: Branch current through 6 Omega resistor:) Tj
T*
(I_6Omega = I1 - I2 = 2.0 - 0.0 = 2.0 Amperes \(flowing downwards\).) Tj
T*
(Check: Voltage drop across 6 Omega = 2.0 A * 6 Omega = 12 V. In Mesh 2, this exactly) Tj
T*
(balances the 12V battery, confirming I2 = 0.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
([SOLVED PROBLEM 2: Thevenin Equivalent and Maximum Power Transfer]) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
/F2 9.5 Tf
(-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
(Problem: For a bridge circuit where terminals A-B are connected across a variable load) Tj
T*
(R_L: A 24V source feeds a parallel divider of \(R1 = 6 Omega, R2 = 3 Omega\) on the left) Tj
T*
(and \(R3 = 4 Omega, R4 = 8 Omega\) on the right. Terminals A and B are between the) Tj
T*
(dividers. Find: \(1\) Thevenin voltage V_th, \(2\) Thevenin resistance R_th, \(3\) Value of) Tj
T*
(R_L for maximum power, \(4\) P_max.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Calculate Open-Circuit Voltage V_th \(V_A - V_B\):) Tj
T*
(V_A = 24 * [ R2 / \(R1 + R2\) ] = 24 * [ 3 / \(6 + 3\) ] = 24 * \(3 / 9\) = 8.0 V.) Tj
T*
(V_B = 24 * [ R4 / \(R3 + R4\) ] = 24 * [ 8 / \(4 + 8\) ] = 24 * \(8 / 12\) = 16.0 V.) Tj
T*
(V_th = | V_A - V_B | = | 8.0 - 16.0 | = 8.0 Volts \(Terminal B is positive relative to) Tj
T*
(A\).) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Calculate Thevenin Resistance R_th \(deactivate 24V voltage source to) Tj
T*
(short circuit\):) Tj
T*
(Seen from terminals A-B, R1 is in parallel with R2, and R3 is in parallel with R4:) Tj
T*
