T*
(* Q1: State and prove the Maximum Power Transfer Theorem for DC networks.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Statement: A resistive network delivers maximum power to a variable load) Tj
T*
(resistance R_L when R_L is equal to the Thevenin equivalent resistance R_th of the) Tj
T*
(network. Proof: Replace the network by its Thevenin equivalent \(V_th in series with) Tj
T*
(R_th\). The load current is I_L = V_th / \(R_th + R_L\). The power delivered to the load is) Tj
T*
(P_L = \(I_L\)^2 * R_L = [ \(V_th\)^2 * R_L ] / \(R_th + R_L\)^2. To find the maximum power,) Tj
T*
(differentiate P_L with respect to R_L and set dP_L / dR_L = 0: dP_L / dR_L = \(V_th\)^2 *) Tj
T*
([ \(R_th + R_L\)^2 * \(1\) - R_L * 2*\(R_th + R_L\) ] / \(R_th + R_L\)^4 = 0. Simplifying) Tj
T*
(numerator: \(R_th + R_L\) - 2*R_L = 0 => R_th - R_L = 0 => R_L = R_th. Hence proved.) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the step-by-step procedure to determine Thevenin's equivalent) Tj
T*
(resistance \(R_th\) when a circuit contains both independent and dependent sources.) Tj
T*
(Answer: When dependent sources are present, R_th cannot be found by simply deactivating) Tj
T*
(sources. Procedure: \(1\) Determine the open-circuit voltage V_oc across terminals A-B) Tj
T*
(using standard nodal or mesh analysis while keeping all sources active. \(2\) Connect an) Tj
T*
(ideal short circuit across A-B and compute the short-circuit current I_sc. \(3\) Thevenin) Tj
T*
(resistance is given by R_th = V_oc / I_sc. Alternatively: Deactivate only the) Tj
T*
(INDEPENDENT sources, connect an external test source \(either V_test = 1V or I_test = 1A\)) Tj
T*
(across terminals A-B, determine the resulting current I_test \(or voltage V_test\), and) Tj
T*
(calculate R_th = V_test / I_test.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
